      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Here’s a concise overview of what water is, how it’s structured, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n## 1. Chemical Identity\n- **Formula:** **H₂O** – each molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom.\n- **Molecular weight:** ≈ 18.015 g/mol.\n- **Bond angle:** ≈ 104.5°, giving the molecule a bent (V‑shaped) geometry.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Physical States\n| State | Typical Conditions | Key Characteristics |\n|-------|--------------------|----------------------|\n| **Solid (Ice)** | ≤ 0 °C (32 °F) | Crystalline lattice, less dense than liquid water (hence ice floats). |\n| **Liquid** | 0 °C – 100 °C (32 °F – 212 °F) at 1 atm | Highly cohesive, excellent solvent, high surface tension, high specific heat capacity. |\n| **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam)** | ≥ 100 °C (212 °F) at 1 atm | Invisible gas; when condensed, forms clouds, fog, or steam. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Unique Physical & Chemical Properties\n| Property | Why It’s Important |\n|----------|--------------------|\n| **Polarity** | The uneven charge distribution makes water a superb solvent for ionic and polar substances. |\n| **Hydrogen bonding** | Weak bonds between water molecules give rise to high boiling point, surface tension, and the anomalous density maximum at 4 °C. |\n| **High specific heat (4.18 J·g⁻¹·K⁻¹)** | Buffers temperature changes, stabilizing climates and biological systems. |\n| **High heat of vaporization (≈ 2260 kJ·kg⁻¹)** | Drives evaporative cooling (e.g., sweating). |\n| **Cohesion & adhesion** | Enables capillary action, crucial for plant water transport. |\n| **Density anomaly** | Ice is ~9 % less dense than liquid water, allowing it to float and insulate aquatic life in winter. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Role in the Environment & Life\n- **Universal solvent:** Dissolves salts, sugars, gases, and many organic compounds, facilitating biochemical reactions.\n- **Hydrologic cycle:** Evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration → groundwater → back to the oceans.\n- **Biological functions:** Constitutes ~60 % of the human body, participates in metabolism, temperature regulation, nutrient transport, and waste removal.\n- **Climate regulation:** Oceans store ~93 % of Earth’s heat; water vapor is a potent greenhouse gas.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Sources & Distribution\n- **Earth’s surface:** ~71 % covered by oceans (saline water). Freshwater is found in glaciers, ice caps, groundwater, lakes, and rivers (~2.5 % of total water, with only ~0.3 % readily accessible for human use).\n- **Atmosphere:** Contains water vapor (~0.25 % of total atmospheric mass) that forms clouds and precipitation.\n- **In living organisms:** Water is the major constituent of cells, tissues, and bodily fluids.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Everyday Examples\n- **Drinking water:** Provides essential hydration and minerals.\n- **Cooking:** Transfers heat efficiently and dissolves flavors.\n- **Cleaning:** Its solvent properties help remove dirt and oils.\n- **Industrial uses:** Coolants, solvents, steam turbines, and chemical synthesis.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Takeaway\nWater is a polar, hydrogen‑bonded molecule (H₂O) that exists naturally as ice, liquid, and vapor. Its exceptional physical properties—high heat capacity, surface tension, solvent power, and density anomaly—make it indispensable for Earth’s climate, ecosystems, and all known forms of life."
